window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["jenkins"]=[{"id":"jenkins-f0001","card":"q-jenkins-001-what-jenkins-is-and-the-devops-lifecycle","sec":"jenkins-s002","q":"What is Jenkins, and what role does it play in a CI/CD pipeline?","a":"<p>Jenkins is an open-source automation server written in Java. In CI/CD it is the orchestrator: it watches source control, builds and tests code on every change (CI), and automates packaging, artifact publishing and deployment through stages to various environments (CD). It ties together compilers, test runners, scanners and deploy tools via plugins and pipelines.</p>\n<p>The DevOps lifecycle is drawn as an infinity symbol: a continuous loop until the system is decommissioned. It has two groups of stages:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Development: Plan, Code, Build, Test.</li>\n  <li>Operational: Release, Deploy, Operate, Monitor.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Jenkins automates the Build, Test, Release and Deploy stages.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Continuous Integration</strong> ties to Build and Test. The goal is to find and fix issues early. Jenkins builds the code into an artifact (container image, Java archive, Windows executable), runs automated tests, and lets the artifact move on only when the tests pass.</li>\n  <li><strong>Continuous Delivery/Deployment</strong> ties to Release and Deploy. Release means uploading artifacts (push a container image to a repository, publish a JAR). Deploy can be manual or fully automated (Continuous Deployment), where Jenkins executes the deployment instructions with little or no human interaction.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pipeline {\n    agent any\n    stages {\n        stage(&#x27;Plan&#x27;) {\n            steps { echo &#x27;Planning the application...&#x27; }\n        }\n        stage(&#x27;Code&#x27;) {\n            steps { git &#x27;https://github.com/example/repo.git&#x27; }\n        }\n        stage(&#x27;Build&#x27;) {\n            steps { sh &#x27;mvn clean package&#x27; }\n        }\n        stage(&#x27;Test&#x27;) {\n            steps { sh &#x27;mvn test&#x27; }\n        }\n        stage(&#x27;Release&#x27;) {\n            steps { sh &#x27;docker push my-app:latest&#x27; }\n        }\n        stage(&#x27;Deploy&#x27;) {\n            steps { sh &#x27;kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml&#x27; }\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0002","card":"q-jenkins-002-ci-vs-continuous-delivery-vs-continuous","sec":"jenkins-s003","q":"What is the difference between Continuous Integration, Continuous Delivery, and Continuous Deployment?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>CI</strong>: developers merge frequently; each change is automatically built and tested.</li>\n  <li><strong>Continuous Delivery</strong>: every passing build is automatically prepared and deployable, but a human approves the release to production.</li>\n  <li><strong>Continuous Deployment</strong>: every passing change is released to production automatically, with no manual gate.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0003","card":"q-jenkins-003-jenkins-vs-github-actions","sec":"jenkins-s004","q":"What are the main advantages of GitHub Actions over tools like Jenkins or CircleCI?","a":"<p>GitHub integration, easy YAML setup, the action marketplace, a free tier and scalable runners.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0004","card":"q-jenkins-004-controller-and-agent-architecture","sec":"jenkins-s005","q":"Explain the controller-agent architecture in Jenkins.","a":"<p>The <strong>controller</strong> hosts the UI, schedules builds, stores config and job history, and manages plugins. <strong>Agents</strong> (nodes) are separate machines or containers that run the build workloads. The controller dispatches jobs to agents based on labels and available executors. Best practice: run no builds on the controller; keep it for orchestration only.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0005","card":"q-jenkins-004-controller-and-agent-architecture","sec":"jenkins-s005","q":"Why should you avoid running build workloads on the controller?","a":"<p>The controller holds all state, credentials and orchestration. Running builds there risks resource exhaustion (OOM/CPU), lets build scripts read sensitive controller files and secrets, and can crash the whole instance. Set controller executors to 0 and run all builds on agents.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0006","card":"q-jenkins-005-executors-labels-and-agent-protocols","sec":"jenkins-s006","q":"What is a Jenkins executor, and how many per agent?","a":"<p>An executor is a slot on a node that runs one build at a time; a node with N executors runs N concurrent builds. Rule of thumb: about one executor per CPU core, fewer for memory-heavy or I/O-heavy builds. Too many causes contention or OOM; too few wastes capacity. Keep the controller's executors at 0.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0007","card":"q-jenkins-005-executors-labels-and-agent-protocols","sec":"jenkins-s006","q":"What are Jenkins labels?","a":"<p>Tags assigned to agents (for example <code>linux</code>, <code>docker</code>, <code>maven</code>). A pipeline requests a label so the build runs only on matching agents. Labels support expressions such as <code>linux &amp;&amp; docker</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pipeline {\n  agent { label &#x27;linux &amp;&amp; docker&#x27; }   // run only on agents with both labels\n  stages { stage(&#x27;Build&#x27;) { steps { sh &#x27;make&#x27; } } }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0008","card":"q-jenkins-005-executors-labels-and-agent-protocols","sec":"jenkins-s006","q":"How does Jenkins authenticate agents, and which protocols are used?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>SSH</strong>: the controller connects out to the agent using SSH credentials (controller-initiated).</li>\n  <li><strong>Inbound (JNLP)</strong>: the agent connects in to the controller using a secret or agent token (agent-initiated; good behind NAT or firewalls).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Agents are validated through these secrets and keys, and you can also lock down which agent commands the controller trusts.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0009","card":"q-jenkins-005-executors-labels-and-agent-protocols","sec":"jenkins-s006","q":"What are the default ports?","a":"<p>Web UI: 8080 (HTTP). The inbound (JNLP) agent port is configured separately (often 50000) under Manage Jenkins, Security; it can be fixed or random.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0010","card":"q-jenkins-006-freestyle-vs-pipeline-jobs-and-plugins","sec":"jenkins-s007","q":"What is the difference between a Freestyle job and a Pipeline job?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Freestyle</strong>: configured in the GUI, roughly single-stage; simple but limited, and the config lives inside Jenkins (harder to version and review).</li>\n  <li><strong>Pipeline</strong>: defined as code in a <code>Jenkinsfile</code> (Groovy); supports multi-stage flows, parallelism, <code>when</code> and <code>input</code>; lives in source control (auditable, reusable, portable).</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0011","card":"q-jenkins-006-freestyle-vs-pipeline-jobs-and-plugins","sec":"jenkins-s007","q":"What are Jenkins plugins, and how do they extend core functionality?","a":"<p>Plugins are add-ons for SCM integrations (Git), build tools, pipeline steps, credential providers, cloud agents (Kubernetes, EC2), notifications and more. Jenkins core is intentionally minimal; almost all real functionality comes from plugins, managed under Manage Jenkins, Plugins.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0012","card":"q-jenkins-007-jenkins-home-configuration-and-backup","sec":"jenkins-s008","q":"What is <code>JENKINS_HOME</code>, and what lives in it?","a":"<p>The root data directory holding all Jenkins state:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>config.xml</code>: global config</li>\n  <li><code>jobs/</code>: per-job config and build history</li>\n  <li><code>plugins/</code>: installed plugins</li>\n  <li><code>secrets/</code>: encryption keys and credential material</li>\n  <li><code>users/</code>, <code>nodes/</code>, <code>workspace/</code>, <code>fingerprints/</code>, <code>updates/</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Backing it up backs up the whole instance.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0013","card":"q-jenkins-007-jenkins-home-configuration-and-backup","sec":"jenkins-s008","q":"How does Jenkins store configuration, and why XML?","a":"<p>Config is persisted as XML files on disk (<code>config.xml</code>, per-job <code>config.xml</code>, node and user configs). Jenkins serializes its Java object model with XStream, so the files are human-readable, diffable and directly editable. That is also why config can be version-controlled and reloaded.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0014","card":"q-jenkins-008-backup-migration-and-restarts","sec":"jenkins-s009","q":"How do you back up and restore a Jenkins instance?","a":"<p>Manual: stop or quiesce Jenkins and archive <code>JENKINS_HOME</code> (at minimum <code>config.xml</code>, <code>jobs/</code>, <code>users/</code>, <code>secrets/</code>, <code>credentials.xml</code>, <code>plugins/</code>). To restore, put it back and restart. With plugins: <strong>ThinBackup</strong> (scheduled full or incremental), or keep config in JCasC plus jobs as code so the instance is reproducible.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0015","card":"q-jenkins-008-backup-migration-and-restarts","sec":"jenkins-s009","q":"How do you move jobs from one Jenkins instance to another?","a":"<p>Copy the job folder under <code>jobs/&lt;name&gt;/</code> (with <code>config.xml</code>) to the new controller and reload; use the Jenkins CLI (<code>get-job</code> / <code>create-job</code>); use the Job Import plugin; or, best, keep jobs as <code>Jenkinsfile</code> and JCasC so migration is just pointing the new controller at the repos.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0016","card":"q-jenkins-008-backup-migration-and-restarts","sec":"jenkins-s009","q":"What is the difference between soft reload (<code>/reload</code>) and safe restart (<code>/safeRestart</code>)?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><code>/reload</code> re-reads config from disk into memory without restarting the JVM (fast; use after editing config on disk).</li>\n  <li><code>/safeRestart</code> waits for running builds to finish, then restarts the JVM, so no builds are killed.</li>\n  <li><code>/restart</code> restarts immediately.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0017","card":"q-jenkins-008-backup-migration-and-restarts","sec":"jenkins-s009","q":"What is the System Log for, and where do you configure custom log recorders?","a":"<p>The System Log surfaces controller and plugin logging for diagnostics. Under Manage Jenkins, System Log you can add custom Log Recorders that capture specific loggers (by package or class) at chosen levels, which helps debug one plugin without drowning in global logs.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0018","card":"q-jenkins-013-scripted-vs-declarative-and-the-required","sec":"jenkins-s014","q":"What are the key differences between Declarative and Scripted Pipeline syntax?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Declarative</strong>: opinionated and structured (<code>pipeline { ... }</code>), easier to read, with built-in <code>post</code>, <code>when</code> and <code>options</code>, validated up front. Preferred for most cases.</li>\n  <li><strong>Scripted</strong>: full Groovy (<code>node { ... }</code>), imperative, maximum flexibility but more complex and error-prone.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>You can drop into scripted logic inside declarative with a <code>script {}</code> block.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>// Declarative\npipeline { agent any; stages { stage(&#x27;B&#x27;){ steps { sh &#x27;make&#x27; } } } }\n// Scripted\nnode { stage(&#x27;B&#x27;) { sh &#x27;make&#x27; } }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0019","card":"q-jenkins-013-scripted-vs-declarative-and-the-required","sec":"jenkins-s014","q":"Explain the core sections of a Declarative Pipeline.","a":"<ul>\n  <li><code>pipeline</code>: the root block.</li>\n  <li><code>agent</code>: where it runs. <code>any</code> (any available executor), <code>label '...'</code> (a specific agent for an OS or tool), <code>docker</code> (inside a container for consistent environments), <code>none</code> (defer agent choice to individual stages).</li>\n  <li><code>stages</code>: container for one or more <code>stage</code>s (at least one required), such as Build, Test, Deploy.</li>\n  <li><code>stage</code>: a named phase.</li>\n  <li><code>steps</code>: the commands that do the work inside a stage (<code>sh</code>, <code>echo</code>); several commands are allowed per step block.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0020","card":"q-jenkins-016-environment-variables-and-build-variable","sec":"jenkins-s017","q":"How do you define environment variables globally and at stage level?","a":"<p>Use an <code>environment</code> block at pipeline level (all stages) or inside a stage (that stage only). Reference with <code>${VAR}</code> or <code>env.VAR</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Name variables in capitals (<code>MAX_SIZE</code>) to tell them apart from lowercase keywords.</li>\n  <li>In strings prefix with <code>$</code> (<code>$VAR</code> or <code>${VAR}</code>), and stay consistent in the style you pick.</li>\n  <li>A stage-level definition overrides the global one for that stage only.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pipeline {\n    agent any\n    environment {\n        MAX_SIZE = &#x27;10&#x27;\n        MIN_SIZE = &#x27;1&#x27;\n    }\n    stages {\n        stage(&#x27;Default Scale&#x27;) {\n            steps { echo &quot;Default: Max size is ${MAX_SIZE}, Min size is ${MIN_SIZE}&quot; }\n        }\n        stage(&#x27;Scale by 10X&#x27;) {\n            environment {\n                MAX_SIZE = &#x27;100&#x27;\n                MIN_SIZE = &#x27;10&#x27;\n            }\n            steps { echo &quot;Scaled: Max size is ${MAX_SIZE}, Min size is ${MIN_SIZE}&quot; }\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>The first stage prints 10 and 1, the second prints 100 and 10. The Pipeline Steps view shows each step's output clearly.</p>\n<p><strong>Current build variables</strong> are properties of the <code>currentBuild</code> object, accessed as <code>currentBuild.PROPERTY</code> (case-sensitive CamelCase): <code>currentBuild.startTime</code>, <code>currentBuild.duration</code>, <code>currentBuild.currentResult</code>. Use them for decisions based on build state, for example <code>echo \"Build duration: ${currentBuild.duration}\"</code>. The full list of default environment and <code>currentBuild</code> variables is under Pipeline Syntax, Global Variables Reference.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0021","card":"q-jenkins-017-the-post-section","sec":"jenkins-s018","q":"What is the <code>post</code> section, and which conditions can it evaluate?","a":"<p><code>post</code> runs after a stage or the whole pipeline based on outcome: <code>always</code>, <code>success</code>, <code>failure</code>, <code>unstable</code>, <code>changed</code> (result differs from the last run), <code>aborted</code>, <code>fixed</code>, <code>regression</code>. It suits notifications and cleanup.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>post {\n  always  { cleanWs() }\n  success { echo &#x27;OK&#x27; }\n  failure { mail to: &#x27;team@x.com&#x27;, subject: &quot;Failed: ${env.JOB_NAME}&quot;, body: &quot;${env.BUILD_URL}&quot; }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0022","card":"q-jenkins-018-the-script-block","sec":"jenkins-s019","q":"What is the <code>script</code> block, and when should you use it?","a":"<p><code>script { }</code> embeds arbitrary Scripted (Groovy) code inside a declarative stage, for loops, complex conditionals or variable manipulation that declarative syntax cannot express. Use it sparingly; prefer shared-library steps for anything reusable.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>steps {\n  script {\n    def services = [&#x27;api&#x27;, &#x27;web&#x27;, &#x27;worker&#x27;]\n    services.each { s -&gt; sh &quot;docker build -t ${s} ./${s}&quot; }\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0023","card":"q-jenkins-019-parallel-stages-and-matrix","sec":"jenkins-s020","q":"How do you run stages in parallel?","a":"<p>Wrap stages in a <code>parallel</code> block so they run concurrently on available executors or agents, which cuts wall-clock time for independent work.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;Tests&#x27;) {\n  parallel {\n    stage(&#x27;Unit&#x27;)        { steps { sh &#x27;make unit&#x27; } }\n    stage(&#x27;Integration&#x27;) { steps { sh &#x27;make integration&#x27; } }\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0024","card":"q-jenkins-019-parallel-stages-and-matrix","sec":"jenkins-s020","q":"What is the <code>matrix</code> directive?","a":"<p>It runs a stage's steps across combinations of <code>axes</code> (for example OS by JDK version) in parallel without repeating stage code, ideal for cross-platform or multi-version testing. <code>excludes</code> skips invalid combinations.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>matrix {\n  axes {\n    axis { name &#x27;OS&#x27;;  values &#x27;linux&#x27;, &#x27;windows&#x27; }\n    axis { name &#x27;JDK&#x27;; values &#x27;11&#x27;, &#x27;17&#x27; }\n  }\n  stages { stage(&#x27;Test&#x27;) { steps { echo &quot;Test on ${OS} JDK${JDK}&quot; } } }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0025","card":"q-jenkins-020-timeout-retry-catcherror-and-platform-st","sec":"jenkins-s021","q":"How do <code>timeout</code>, <code>retry</code> and <code>catchError</code> work?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><code>timeout</code>: abort a block that exceeds a duration.</li>\n  <li><code>retry(n)</code>: re-run a block up to n times on failure.</li>\n  <li><code>catchError</code>: continue the pipeline but set the build result (for example UNSTABLE) instead of failing hard.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>steps {\n  retry(3) { timeout(time: 5, unit: &#x27;MINUTES&#x27;) { sh &#x27;./flaky.sh&#x27; } }\n  catchError(buildResult: &#x27;UNSTABLE&#x27;, stageResult: &#x27;FAILURE&#x27;) { sh &#x27;./optional.sh&#x27; }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0026","card":"q-jenkins-020-timeout-retry-catcherror-and-platform-st","sec":"jenkins-s021","q":"What is the difference between <code>sh</code>, <code>bat</code> and <code>powershell</code> steps?","a":"<p><code>sh</code> runs shell commands on Unix, Linux and macOS agents; <code>bat</code> runs Windows batch and <code>powershell</code> runs PowerShell on Windows agents. Pick by agent OS (target it with labels): <code>sh</code> will not work on a Windows agent and vice versa.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;Build&#x27;) {\n  steps {\n    sh &#x27;mvn -B clean package&#x27;          // Linux agent\n    // bat &#x27;mvn -B clean package&#x27;      // Windows agent\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0027","card":"q-jenkins-021-replay-and-restart-from-a-stage","sec":"jenkins-s022","q":"How do you replay or restart a build from a failed stage?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Replay</strong> re-runs a build with an editable copy of the pipeline script (good for quick experiments).</li>\n  <li><strong>Restart from Stage</strong> (declarative pipelines, with <code>preserveStashes</code>) restarts a completed run from a chosen stage, reusing earlier stashed artifacts so earlier successful stages are not repeated.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both are available from the build page in Blue Ocean or the classic UI.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>options { preserveStashes(buildCount: 5) }   // enables reliable restart-from-stage</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0028","card":"q-jenkins-023-first-build-and-build-with-parameters","sec":"jenkins-s024","q":"How do you configure parameterised builds (String, Choice, Boolean, Git Parameter)?","a":"<p>Declare a <code>parameters</code> block; values are supplied at build time and read via <code>params.NAME</code>. The Git Parameter plugin adds a <code>gitParameter</code> type for choosing branches or tags.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>parameters {\n  string(name: &#x27;VERSION&#x27;, defaultValue: &#x27;1.0.0&#x27;, description: &#x27;Release version&#x27;)\n  choice(name: &#x27;ENVIRONMENT&#x27;, choices: [&#x27;dev&#x27;,&#x27;staging&#x27;,&#x27;prod&#x27;], description: &#x27;Target&#x27;)\n  booleanParam(name: &#x27;RUN_TESTS&#x27;, defaultValue: true, description: &#x27;Run tests?&#x27;)\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0029","card":"q-jenkins-024-conditional-stages-with-when","sec":"jenkins-s025","q":"How do you run a stage conditionally with <code>when</code>?","a":"<p><code>when</code> decides whether a stage runs. If it evaluates to true the stage runs, otherwise it is skipped. Conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>branch</code>: only for given Git branches (for example <code>main</code>).</li>\n  <li><code>environment</code>: an environment variable has a given value.</li>\n  <li><code>expression</code>: a Groovy expression using parameters or variables (for example <code>params.ENV != 'prod'</code>).</li>\n  <li>Others include <code>changeRequest</code> and <code>tag</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;Deploy Prod&#x27;) {\n  when { branch &#x27;main&#x27;; expression { params.ENVIRONMENT == &#x27;prod&#x27; } }\n  steps { echo &#x27;Deploying to production&#x27; }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0030","card":"q-jenkins-025-manual-approvals-with-input","sec":"jenkins-s026","q":"How do you require a manual approval before production?","a":"<p>The <code>input</code> step pauses the pipeline for a human to Proceed or Abort, with a custom message to explain the action. Wrap it in <code>timeout</code> so it cannot hang forever.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;Approve&#x27;) {\n  steps {\n    timeout(time: 15, unit: &#x27;MINUTES&#x27;) {\n      input message: &#x27;Deploy to production?&#x27;, ok: &#x27;Deploy&#x27;\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>The paused stage shows a confirmation dialog when you hover over it in the pipeline view.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0031","card":"q-jenkins-028-jenkinsfile-and-pipeline-as-code","sec":"jenkins-s029","q":"What is a Jenkinsfile, and why store it in source control?","a":"<p>A <code>Jenkinsfile</code> is a text file defining the pipeline, committed alongside the code. Benefits: version control and code review, auditable history, reproducibility across branches and environments, self-documenting pipelines, and Multibranch pipelines that build every branch and PR automatically. It also supports a <strong>GitOps</strong> approach where the repository is the single source of truth for application code, tool configuration and CI changes, which can be reviewed and merged through automated processes. A Jenkinsfile can hold tools, options, triggers and more.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0032","card":"q-jenkins-030-ways-to-trigger-a-job","sec":"jenkins-s031","q":"What are the ways to trigger a Jenkins job?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Webhooks</strong>: the SCM pushes an event and the build starts instantly (best).</li>\n  <li><strong>Poll SCM</strong>: Jenkins periodically checks the repo for changes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Scheduled (cron)</strong>: build on a time schedule regardless of changes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Remote API or token</strong>: trigger over HTTP (<code>buildWithParameters</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Upstream</strong>: build after another project completes.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>triggers {\n  githubPush()                 // webhook\n  pollSCM(&#x27;H/5 * * * *&#x27;)       // poll every ~5 min\n  cron(&#x27;H 2 * * *&#x27;)            // nightly ~2am\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0033","card":"q-jenkins-030-ways-to-trigger-a-job","sec":"jenkins-s031","q":"What is the difference between <code>pollSCM</code> and <code>cron</code>?","a":"<p><code>pollSCM('schedule')</code> checks SCM on that schedule and builds only if there are changes. <code>cron('schedule')</code> builds unconditionally on the schedule (nightly jobs). Use <code>H</code> (hash) to spread load and avoid every job firing on the hour.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0034","card":"q-jenkins-030-ways-to-trigger-a-job","sec":"jenkins-s031","q":"How do GitHub or GitLab webhooks trigger builds instantly?","a":"<p>The SCM sends an HTTP POST to Jenkins (<code>/github-webhook/</code> or the GitLab hook URL) on push or PR events. Jenkins matches it to jobs watching that repo and builds immediately, which is faster and cheaper than polling.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0035","card":"q-jenkins-031-multibranch-pipelines-and-organization-f","sec":"jenkins-s032","q":"What is a Multibranch Pipeline?","a":"<p>It scans a repo and creates and runs a pipeline for every branch and PR that contains a <code>Jenkinsfile</code>. New branches create new jobs and deleted branches remove them. Each branch builds with its own Jenkinsfile, so per-branch CI needs no manual job setup.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0036","card":"q-jenkins-031-multibranch-pipelines-and-organization-f","sec":"jenkins-s032","q":"What is the GitHub Organization Folder or GitLab Group plugin?","a":"<p>They scan an entire GitHub org or GitLab group and auto-create a Multibranch Pipeline for every repo containing a Jenkinsfile. New repos are picked up on the next scan or webhook.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0037","card":"q-jenkins-031-multibranch-pipelines-and-organization-f","sec":"jenkins-s032","q":"How do you build pull requests and report status back?","a":"<p>Use a Multibranch Pipeline with the GitHub or GitLab Branch Source: it discovers PRs and builds them, and Jenkins reports commit status (pending, success, failure) to the PR automatically (or with <code>githubNotify</code> or the GitLab status). Combine with branch protection to require the check before merge.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0038","card":"q-jenkins-032-downstream-jobs-remote-triggers-and-webh","sec":"jenkins-s033","q":"How do you trigger downstream jobs and pass parameters?","a":"<p>Use the <code>build</code> step with <code>parameters</code>. <code>wait</code> and <code>propagate</code> control blocking and result propagation.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>build job: &#x27;deploy-service&#x27;,\n  parameters: [string(name: &#x27;VERSION&#x27;, value: env.BUILD_NUMBER)],\n  wait: true, propagate: true</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0039","card":"q-jenkins-032-downstream-jobs-remote-triggers-and-webh","sec":"jenkins-s033","q":"How do you trigger a build remotely with cURL and an API token?","a":"<p>Create a user API token, then POST to the job's <code>build</code> or <code>buildWithParameters</code> endpoint (with a crumb if CSRF protection is on). Trigger tokens can also be set per job.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>curl -X POST &quot;https://jenkins.example.com/job/my-job/buildWithParameters?VERSION=1.2.3&quot; \\\n  --user &quot;alice:11aabbccddeeff...&quot;   # user:apiToken</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0040","card":"q-jenkins-032-downstream-jobs-remote-triggers-and-webh","sec":"jenkins-s033","q":"What is the Generic Webhook Trigger plugin?","a":"<p>It lets any system trigger a job through one webhook URL and extracts fields from the JSON or XML payload into variables with JSONPath. The variables can feed an optional filter that decides whether to build.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>triggers {\n  GenericTrigger(\n    genericVariables: [[key: &#x27;ref&#x27;, value: &#x27;$.ref&#x27;]],\n    token: &#x27;my-token&#x27;,\n    regexpFilterText: &#x27;$ref&#x27;, regexpFilterExpression: &#x27;refs/heads/main&#x27;\n  )\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0041","card":"q-jenkins-033-checkout-optimization-concurrency-and-su","sec":"jenkins-s034","q":"What is Git sparse checkout, and how do you speed up large checkouts?","a":"<p>Sparse checkout fetches only specified paths instead of the whole repo, which helps monorepos. Other options: shallow clone (<code>depth</code>), skipping tags, reference repos, and disabling unneeded submodules.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>checkout([$class: &#x27;GitSCM&#x27;,\n  branches: [[name: &#x27;*/main&#x27;]],\n  extensions: [\n    [$class: &#x27;CloneOption&#x27;, depth: 1, shallow: true, noTags: true],\n    [$class: &#x27;SparseCheckoutPaths&#x27;, sparseCheckoutPaths: [[path: &#x27;services/api/&#x27;]]]\n  ],\n  userRemoteConfigs: [[url: &#x27;https://github.com/org/monorepo.git&#x27;]]\n])</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0042","card":"q-jenkins-033-checkout-optimization-concurrency-and-su","sec":"jenkins-s034","q":"How do you handle Git submodules during checkout?","a":"<p>Enable the <code>SubmoduleOption</code> extension (recursive, with parent credentials) so submodules are initialized and updated.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>checkout([$class: &#x27;GitSCM&#x27;, branches: [[name: &#x27;*/main&#x27;]],\n  extensions: [[$class: &#x27;SubmoduleOption&#x27;, recursiveSubmodules: true, parentCredentials: true]],\n  userRemoteConfigs: [[url: &#x27;https://github.com/org/repo.git&#x27;]]])</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0043","card":"q-jenkins-033-checkout-optimization-concurrency-and-su","sec":"jenkins-s034","q":"How do you stop concurrent builds of the same branch?","a":"<p>Add <code>disableConcurrentBuilds</code>: a new build of the same job or branch queues until the current one finishes, avoiding races on shared resources and deploys. <code>abortPrevious: true</code> cancels the older run instead.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>options { disableConcurrentBuilds() }\n// or: options { disableConcurrentBuilds(abortPrevious: true) }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0044","card":"q-jenkins-033-checkout-optimization-concurrency-and-su","sec":"jenkins-s034","q":"What is the Quiet Period?","a":"<p>A configurable delay between a trigger firing and the build starting. It batches a burst of rapid commits into one build and avoids redundant runs.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0045","card":"q-jenkins-039-default-security-realms-and-authorizatio","sec":"jenkins-s040","q":"How do you integrate Jenkins with LDAP, Active Directory or SAML/Okta for SSO?","a":"<p>Set the Security Realm to LDAP, Active Directory, or a SAML/OIDC plugin. Jenkins delegates authentication to the identity provider, and group memberships flow in and map to authorization roles or the matrix. This centralizes identity and enables SSO.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0046","card":"q-jenkins-040-matrix-based-security","sec":"jenkins-s041","q":"What is the difference between matrix-based security and Role-Based Access Control (Role Strategy plugin)?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Matrix-based</strong>: a global grid mapping users and groups to permissions. Simple, but it does not scale because every user needs a row.</li>\n  <li><strong>Role Strategy (RBAC)</strong>: define reusable roles (global plus per-project or folder patterns) and assign users or groups to them; far more scalable for many users and teams.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Configure matrix security:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Manage Jenkins, Manage Users: create users (username, password, name, email).</li>\n  <li>Manage Jenkins, Configure Global Security: switch from \"Logged-in users can do anything\" to <strong>Matrix-based security</strong>.</li>\n  <li>Add your admin account and tick <strong>Administer</strong>. Add other users with specific permissions such as Overall Read (interact with the UI) and Job Build, Cancel, Read.</li>\n  <li>Save, then log in as the restricted user and confirm there is no management menu and only limited job actions.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Best practice: limit admin access to one or two users on a shared system and give everyone else role-based permissions. Always grant your own account Administer before saving, because locking yourself out is common (the Jenkins docs explain how to disable access control to recover).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0047","card":"q-jenkins-043-credentials-store-and-scopes","sec":"jenkins-s044","q":"What is the Credentials provider, and which types can it store?","a":"<p>The Credentials plugin stores secrets encrypted in Jenkins and exposes them to jobs by ID, never as plain text. Types: username and password, SSH private key, secret text (API keys, tokens), secret file and certificates. Credentials can be domain-restricted.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0048","card":"q-jenkins-043-credentials-store-and-scopes","sec":"jenkins-s044","q":"What is the difference between credential scopes?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Global</strong>: usable by any job or pipeline on the instance.</li>\n  <li><strong>System</strong>: usable only by Jenkins itself (connecting to agents, email), not exposed to job builds.</li>\n  <li><strong>Item/Folder</strong>: scoped to one folder or job (least privilege, good for team isolation).</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0049","card":"q-jenkins-044-using-credentials-in-a-jenkinsfile","sec":"jenkins-s045","q":"How do you inject credentials securely?","a":"<p><code>withCredentials</code> binds a stored credential to environment variables for the duration of a block, then unbinds it, and Jenkins masks the values in logs. Alternatively use <code>credentials('id')</code> in an <code>environment</code> block. For username and password credentials it sets one variable holding <code>username:password</code> plus two derived variables, <code>&lt;NAME&gt;_USR</code> and <code>&lt;NAME&gt;_PSW</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>withCredentials([usernamePassword(credentialsId: &#x27;dockerhub&#x27;,\n    usernameVariable: &#x27;USER&#x27;, passwordVariable: &#x27;PASS&#x27;)]) {\n  sh &#x27;echo &quot;$PASS&quot; | docker login -u &quot;$USER&quot; --password-stdin&#x27;\n}\n// or environment block:\nenvironment { AWS_KEY = credentials(&#x27;aws-access-key&#x27;) }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pipeline {\n    agent any\n    environment {\n        MY_CRED = credentials(&#x27;my-credential-id&#x27;)   // username:password\n    }\n    stages {\n        stage(&#x27;Example&#x27;) {\n            steps {\n                echo &quot;Username: ${env.MY_CRED_USR}&quot;  // MY_CRED_PSW holds the password\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>// Secret text with withCredentials\nwithCredentials([string(credentialsId: &#x27;my-api-key&#x27;, variable: &#x27;API_KEY&#x27;)]) {\n    sh &#x27;echo $API_KEY&#x27;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Use the string form of <code>withCredentials</code> for secret text credentials. Recreate the permission matrix when switching strategies and test as a restricted user.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0050","card":"q-jenkins-045-secrets-hygiene-ssh-agent-and-vault","sec":"jenkins-s046","q":"Why never hardcode tokens or passwords in steps, and how do you mask output?","a":"<p>Hardcoded secrets leak into SCM, build logs and job config and cannot be rotated centrally. Use the Credentials store with <code>withCredentials</code> (Jenkins masks bound values in logs). The Mask Passwords plugin can redact custom secret strings. Do not <code>echo</code> secrets.</p>\n<p>Several credentials can be bound in one <code>withCredentials</code> block:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>withCredentials([\n  usernamePassword(credentialsId: &#x27;docker&#x27;, usernameVariable: &#x27;U&#x27;, passwordVariable: &#x27;P&#x27;),\n  string(credentialsId: &#x27;npm-token&#x27;, variable: &#x27;NPM_TOKEN&#x27;)\n]) {\n  sh &#x27;echo &quot;$P&quot; | docker login -u &quot;$U&quot; --password-stdin&#x27;\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0051","card":"q-jenkins-045-secrets-hygiene-ssh-agent-and-vault","sec":"jenkins-s046","q":"What does the SSH Agent plugin do?","a":"<p>It starts an <code>ssh-agent</code> for a build and loads a stored SSH private-key credential into it, so <code>git</code>, <code>ssh</code> and <code>scp</code> authenticate without the key being written to disk. The key is available only inside the wrapped block.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sshagent([&#x27;deploy-ssh-key&#x27;]) {\n  sh &#x27;ssh deploy@server &quot;systemctl restart app&quot;&#x27;\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0052","card":"q-jenkins-045-secrets-hygiene-ssh-agent-and-vault","sec":"jenkins-s046","q":"How do you integrate Jenkins with Vault or AWS Secrets Manager?","a":"<p>Use the HashiCorp Vault or AWS Secrets Manager Credentials Provider plugin. Secrets stay in the external manager (single source of truth, rotation, audit) and are fetched at runtime, so there are no long-lived copies in Jenkins.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>withVault(vaultSecrets: [[path: &#x27;secret/app&#x27;, secretValues: [[envVar: &#x27;DB_PASS&#x27;, vaultKey: &#x27;password&#x27;]]]]) {\n  sh &#x27;deploy --password &quot;$DB_PASS&quot;&#x27;\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0053","card":"q-jenkins-046-script-security-csrf-and-hardening","sec":"jenkins-s047","q":"What is Script Security, and why do some Groovy scripts need admin approval?","a":"<p>The Script Security plugin gates Groovy that runs on the controller. Sandboxed pipeline code is limited to safe APIs; anything outside the sandbox (or <code>@NonCPS</code> code using sensitive methods) goes to In-process Script Approval, where an admin must approve it. This stops arbitrary code from compromising the controller.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0054","card":"q-jenkins-046-script-security-csrf-and-hardening","sec":"jenkins-s047","q":"How do you restrict script capabilities with the Groovy Sandbox?","a":"<p>Run pipeline Groovy in the sandbox (the default for a Jenkinsfile from SCM). Only allowlisted, safe methods are allowed; anything else is blocked or needs admin approval, so pipeline code cannot call dangerous controller APIs or the filesystem directly.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0055","card":"q-jenkins-047-csrf-agent-security-and-auditing","sec":"jenkins-s048","q":"How do you protect the controller against CSRF?","a":"<p>Enable CSRF protection with the crumb issuer (on by default in modern Jenkins). State-changing requests must carry a valid crumb, which blocks forged cross-site requests. API clients fetch a crumb first, or use API tokens, which are crumb-exempt for scripted calls.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0056","card":"q-jenkins-047-csrf-agent-security-and-auditing","sec":"jenkins-s048","q":"What is Agent-to-Controller security?","a":"<p>Agents (possibly less trusted) can ask the controller to run commands or read files. Agent-to-Controller security restricts this with a configurable allow and deny list of commands and file access, which limits the blast radius if an agent is compromised.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0057","card":"q-jenkins-047-csrf-agent-security-and-auditing","sec":"jenkins-s048","q":"How do you disable anonymous read access?","a":"<p>In the authorization strategy (matrix or role), remove all permissions from the Anonymous user (do not grant Overall Read). Then only authenticated users can view Jenkins. Create a real admin account first so you do not lock yourself out.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0058","card":"q-jenkins-047-csrf-agent-security-and-auditing","sec":"jenkins-s048","q":"How do you audit user actions and job changes?","a":"<p>Install the <strong>Audit Trail</strong> plugin to log who did what (config changes, triggers, deletions) to a file, syslog or console. Combine it with <strong>Job Configuration History</strong> to track and diff config changes over time.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0059","card":"q-jenkins-047-csrf-agent-security-and-auditing","sec":"jenkins-s048","q":"What security considerations apply to untrusted code from GitHub PRs?","a":"<p>PRs from forks can carry a malicious Jenkinsfile or build scripts. Require approval to build fork PRs (use the trusted strategy under \"Discover PRs from forks\"), never expose production credentials to PR builds, run PR builds on isolated ephemeral agents, use least-privilege scoped credentials, and avoid sandbox bypass. Treat PR code as hostile.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0060","card":"q-jenkins-049-pipeline-considerations-for-agents","sec":"jenkins-s050","q":"How do you manage tool installations across different agents?","a":"<p>Define installers in Global Tool Configuration and reference them with a <code>tools</code> block.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pipeline {\n  agent any\n  tools { maven &#x27;Maven-3.9&#x27;; jdk &#x27;JDK-17&#x27; }   // names from Global Tool Config\n  stages { stage(&#x27;Build&#x27;) { steps { sh &#x27;mvn -v &amp;&amp; mvn package&#x27; } } }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0061","card":"q-jenkins-053-kubernetes-and-cloud-agents","sec":"jenkins-s054","q":"What is the Kubernetes plugin, and how does it spin up agents?","a":"<p>It runs each build in an ephemeral pod created on demand in a cluster and deletes it afterwards. You define a cloud pointing at the cluster and pod templates, and Jenkins scales agents elastically with zero idle cost, ideal for bursty CI.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>agent {\n  kubernetes {\n    yaml &#x27;&#x27;&#x27;\n      apiVersion: v1\n      kind: Pod\n      spec:\n        containers:\n        - name: maven\n          image: maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-17\n          command: [&#x27;sleep&#x27;]\n          args: [&#x27;9999999&#x27;]\n    &#x27;&#x27;&#x27;\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0062","card":"q-jenkins-054-ephemeral-agents","sec":"jenkins-s055","q":"Why are dynamic ephemeral agents ideal for the cloud?","a":"<p>They are created per build and destroyed after, so there are no long-lived nodes to patch and no leaked state. Benefits: a clean reproducible environment per run, elastic scale (pay only while building) and no snowflake agents. They suit Kubernetes and EC2, where compute is on demand.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0063","card":"q-jenkins-055-multi-container-pods-ec2-agents-and-kani","sec":"jenkins-s056","q":"How do you run several containers (Maven, AWS CLI) in one pod?","a":"<p>Declare multiple containers in the pod YAML and target each with <code>container('name')</code>; they share the workspace volume.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>agent {\n  kubernetes { yaml &#x27;&#x27;&#x27;\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - {name: maven, image: maven:3.9, command: [cat], tty: true}\n      - {name: aws,   image: amazon/aws-cli, command: [cat], tty: true}\n  &#x27;&#x27;&#x27; }\n}\nstages {\n  stage(&#x27;Build&#x27;)  { steps { container(&#x27;maven&#x27;) { sh &#x27;mvn package&#x27; } } }\n  stage(&#x27;Deploy&#x27;) { steps { container(&#x27;aws&#x27;)   { sh &#x27;aws s3 cp target/app.jar s3://bucket/&#x27; } } }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0064","card":"q-jenkins-055-multi-container-pods-ec2-agents-and-kani","sec":"jenkins-s056","q":"How do you provision EC2 instances as agents with the AWS EC2 plugin?","a":"<p>Configure an EC2 cloud with an AMI, instance type, IAM role, subnet and security group, and labels. When jobs need capacity Jenkins launches EC2 instances as agents (over SSH) and terminates idle ones per the retention strategy.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0065","card":"q-jenkins-055-multi-container-pods-ec2-agents-and-kani","sec":"jenkins-s056","q":"How does workspace persistence work in Kubernetes pods?","a":"<p>By default an ephemeral pod's workspace vanishes with the pod. Mount a PVC (or use <code>workspaceVolume: dynamicPVC(...)</code>) so the workspace persists across steps, containers or reruns, which helps caches such as <code>.m2</code> and <code>node_modules</code>. Trade-offs: PVC lifecycle and cleanup, and RWX access constraints.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0066","card":"q-jenkins-056-building-images-in-kubernetes-agents","sec":"jenkins-s057","q":"How do you run Docker-in-Docker or Kaniko in Kubernetes agents to build images?","a":"<p>DinD runs a Docker daemon in a sidecar and needs privileged mode, which is a security concern. Kaniko builds images in userspace without a Docker daemon or privileged access, so it is preferred in Kubernetes. It pushes straight to a registry using mounted credentials.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>container(&#x27;kaniko&#x27;) {\n  sh &#x27;/kaniko/executor --context `pwd` --dockerfile Dockerfile --destination registry/app:$BUILD_NUMBER&#x27;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>A pod that builds with Maven and then pushes an image with Kaniko (the <code>debug</code> executor image provides a shell):</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pipeline {\n  agent { kubernetes { yaml &#x27;&#x27;&#x27;\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - {name: maven,  image: maven:3.9,       command: [cat], tty: true}\n      - {name: kaniko, image: gcr.io/kaniko-project/executor:debug, command: [cat], tty: true}\n  &#x27;&#x27;&#x27; } }\n  stages {\n    stage(&#x27;Build&#x27;) { steps { container(&#x27;maven&#x27;)  { sh &#x27;mvn package&#x27; } } }\n    stage(&#x27;Image&#x27;) { steps { container(&#x27;kaniko&#x27;) {\n      sh &#x27;/kaniko/executor --context `pwd` --dockerfile Dockerfile --destination reg/app:$BUILD_NUMBER&#x27;\n    } } }\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0067","card":"q-jenkins-057-launch-methods-retention-and-offline-nod","sec":"jenkins-s058","q":"What is the difference between launch methods?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Launch agent via SSH</strong>: the controller SSHes out to start the agent. Simple for Linux hosts the controller can reach.</li>\n  <li><strong>Launch agent by connecting it to the controller (Inbound/JNLP)</strong>: the agent runs <code>agent.jar</code> and initiates the connection. Good behind NAT or firewalls, and for Windows.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cloud plugins</strong>: Kubernetes, EC2 or Docker create agents on demand.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The choice depends on network reachability, OS and whether agents are static or dynamic.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0068","card":"q-jenkins-057-launch-methods-retention-and-offline-nod","sec":"jenkins-s058","q":"How do you set retention strategies for dynamic agents?","a":"<p>They control the agent lifecycle: <strong>Always</strong> (keep up), <strong>Demand</strong> (start when needed), <strong>Idle timeout</strong> (terminate after N idle minutes), or plugin-specific strategies. Short idle timeouts minimize cloud cost, and a small warm pool reduces cold-start latency.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0069","card":"q-jenkins-057-launch-methods-retention-and-offline-nod","sec":"jenkins-s058","q":"What does Node Offline mean, and what causes disconnects?","a":"<p>The controller lost contact with a node and cannot schedule builds there. Common causes: network or firewall issues, agent process crash or OOM, disk full on the agent, Java version mismatch, an expired credential or secret, or the node being taken offline for maintenance.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0070","card":"q-jenkins-058-what-a-shared-library-is","sec":"jenkins-s059","q":"What is a Shared Library, and why use it in enterprises?","a":"<p>A versioned Git repo of reusable pipeline code (steps, helpers, classes) that many Jenkinsfiles import. It enforces DRY, standardizes CI/CD across teams (one place to fix or upgrade), hides complexity behind simple custom steps and keeps each Jenkinsfile tiny.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0071","card":"q-jenkins-058-what-a-shared-library-is","sec":"jenkins-s059","q":"What is the structure of a Shared Library?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><code>vars/</code>: global custom steps and variables; each <code>foo.groovy</code> becomes a callable step <code>foo(...)</code>.</li>\n  <li><code>src/</code>: Groovy classes in package folders for OOP-style helper logic.</li>\n  <li><code>resources/</code>: non-Groovy files (templates, JSON, scripts) loaded with <code>libraryResource</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Text</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>(root)\n├── vars/          # deployApp.groovy  → step: deployApp(...)\n├── src/           # org/acme/Utils.groovy\n└── resources/     # org/acme/config.json</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0072","card":"q-jenkins-059-loading-a-library-and-writing-a-custom-s","sec":"jenkins-s060","q":"How do you load a library implicitly vs explicitly?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Implicit</strong>: a Global Shared Library configured with \"Load implicitly\" is available in every pipeline.</li>\n  <li><strong>Explicit</strong>: <code>@Library('my-lib@main') _</code> at the top of the Jenkinsfile, pinning a branch, tag or commit.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>@Library(&#x27;shared-pipeline@v2.1&#x27;) _\npipeline { agent any; stages { stage(&#x27;Deploy&#x27;){ steps { deployApp env: &#x27;prod&#x27; } } } }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0073","card":"q-jenkins-059-loading-a-library-and-writing-a-custom-s","sec":"jenkins-s060","q":"How do you write a custom step in <code>vars/</code> with an argument map?","a":"<p>Define a <code>call</code> method taking a <code>Map</code> of named arguments with defaults; the file name becomes the step name.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>// vars/deployApp.groovy\ndef call(Map args = [:]) {\n  String env     = args.env ?: &#x27;dev&#x27;\n  String version = args.version ?: env.BUILD_NUMBER\n  echo &quot;Deploying ${version} to ${env}&quot;\n  sh &quot;./deploy.sh ${env} ${version}&quot;\n}\n// usage: deployApp env: &#x27;prod&#x27;, version: &#x27;1.4.0&#x27;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0074","card":"q-jenkins-059-loading-a-library-and-writing-a-custom-s","sec":"jenkins-s060","q":"What is the difference between <code>vars/*.groovy</code> globals and <code>src/</code> classes?","a":"<p><code>vars</code> files expose a step-like global (with <code>call()</code> making it invocable as <code>name(args)</code>), good for the pipeline-facing DSL. <code>src</code> classes are regular Groovy or OOP for structured, reusable and unit-testable logic. Use <code>vars</code> for the API surface and <code>src</code> for the implementation.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0075","card":"q-jenkins-059-loading-a-library-and-writing-a-custom-s","sec":"jenkins-s060","q":"What is the difference between Global and Folder-level Shared Libraries?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Global</strong>: configured in Manage Jenkins, available instance-wide, can be trusted (runs outside the sandbox) and loaded implicitly.</li>\n  <li><strong>Folder-level</strong>: configured on a folder, scoped to jobs in it, always sandboxed and untrusted. Use for team-specific libraries and to limit the blast radius.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0076","card":"q-jenkins-060-groovy-in-pipelines-cps-and-noncps","sec":"jenkins-s061","q":"Which Groovy concepts matter for robust pipelines (CPS, <code>@NonCPS</code>)?","a":"<p>Pipeline Groovy is CPS-transformed (continuation-passing style) so builds can pause, resume and survive controller restarts. That means program state must be serializable and some Groovy features behave oddly. Mark methods <code>@NonCPS</code> when they do complex non-pausing work (iterating collections with closures, using non-serializable objects), and keep pipeline steps out of them.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>@NonCPS\ndef parse(String json) { new groovy.json.JsonSlurper().parseText(json) }  // runs outside CPS</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0077","card":"q-jenkins-060-groovy-in-pipelines-cps-and-noncps","sec":"jenkins-s061","q":"What causes <code>NotSerializableException</code>, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>CPS serializes pipeline state at every step, so holding a non-serializable object (a <code>JsonSlurper</code> result type, a <code>Matcher</code>, streams) across a step boundary throws it. Fix by confining such objects to a <code>@NonCPS</code> method, nulling them before a step, or copying into serializable types (<code>HashMap</code>, <code>String</code>) before continuing.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0078","card":"q-jenkins-061-testing-and-versioning-libraries","sec":"jenkins-s062","q":"How do you unit test shared libraries?","a":"<p><code>JenkinsPipelineUnit</code> mocks the pipeline runtime so library code runs in plain JUnit or Spock tests, asserting which steps were called and with which arguments, without a live Jenkins. Register mock steps, load the library, execute and verify the call stack.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>// Spock-style\ndef &quot;deployApp echoes target&quot;() {\n  when:  helper.callClosure(&#x27;deployApp&#x27;, [env: &#x27;prod&#x27;])\n  then:  helper.callStack.any { it.methodName == &#x27;echo&#x27; &amp;&amp; it.args[0].contains(&#x27;prod&#x27;) }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0079","card":"q-jenkins-061-testing-and-versioning-libraries","sec":"jenkins-s062","q":"How do you version and test library changes without breaking pipelines?","a":"<p>Keep the library in Git and pin versions in <code>@Library('lib@v1.3')</code> so consumers do not auto-adopt breaking changes. Develop on a branch, test with <code>@Library('lib@feature-x')</code> in a sandbox job (and JenkinsPipelineUnit), then release a tag and bump consumers deliberately. Semantic versioning and a changelog keep upgrades safe.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0080","card":"q-jenkins-062-archiving-artifacts","sec":"jenkins-s063","q":"How do you archive artifacts, and what is the difference between artifacts and stashes?","a":"<p><code>archiveArtifacts</code> saves files to the controller, viewable and downloadable from the build page for the build's retention. A <strong>stash</strong> is temporary in-build storage for passing files between stages or agents and is deleted when the build ends. Archive for deliverables, stash for hand-offs within the pipeline.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>archiveArtifacts artifacts: &#x27;target/*.jar&#x27;, fingerprint: true</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0081","card":"q-jenkins-062-archiving-artifacts","sec":"jenkins-s063","q":"What is the difference between <code>stash</code>/<code>unstash</code> and <code>archiveArtifacts</code>?","a":"<p><code>stash</code>/<code>unstash</code> move files between stages or agents within one build (ephemeral, not shown in the UI). <code>archiveArtifacts</code> persists files to the controller and exposes them in the UI and API.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;Build&#x27;) { steps { sh &#x27;make&#x27;; stash name: &#x27;app&#x27;, includes: &#x27;dist/**&#x27; } }\nstage(&#x27;Deploy&#x27;){ agent { label &#x27;deployer&#x27; }; steps { unstash &#x27;app&#x27;; sh &#x27;./deploy.sh&#x27; } }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0082","card":"q-jenkins-065-junit-reports-and-code-coverage","sec":"jenkins-s066","q":"How do you publish JUnit results and coverage to the UI?","a":"<p>Use <code>junit</code> for test results (trends, and the build goes UNSTABLE on failures) and the coverage plugin (<code>recordCoverage</code>, or <code>publishHTML</code> for HTML reports).</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>post {\n  always {\n    junit &#x27;target/surefire-reports/*.xml&#x27;\n    recordCoverage tools: [[parser: &#x27;JACOCO&#x27;, pattern: &#x27;target/site/jacoco/jacoco.xml&#x27;]]\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0083","card":"q-jenkins-069-jenkins-cli","sec":"jenkins-s070","q":"What is the Jenkins CLI, and how do you run management commands?","a":"<p><code>jenkins-cli.jar</code> runs admin and job commands from a terminal or script: create and build jobs, manage nodes, reload config, safe-restart. Authenticate with an API token, over SSH or the HTTP endpoint.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>java -jar jenkins-cli.jar -s https://jenkins.example.com -auth alice:TOKEN build my-job -f -v\njava -jar jenkins-cli.jar -s https://jenkins.example.com -auth alice:TOKEN safe-restart</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0084","card":"q-jenkins-070-configuration-as-code-jcasc","sec":"jenkins-s071","q":"What is JCasC, and how does it replace manual UI administration?","a":"<p>The <code>configuration-as-code</code> plugin defines the whole controller configuration (security realm, clouds, tools, credentials, global settings) in a declarative YAML file. Instead of clicking through Manage Jenkins, you version the YAML and apply it, which makes the controller reproducible, reviewable and recoverable.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>jenkins:\n  systemMessage: &quot;Managed by JCasC&quot;\n  numExecutors: 0\n  securityRealm:\n    local: { allowsSignup: false }\n  authorizationStrategy:\n    roleBased: { roles: {} }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0085","card":"q-jenkins-070-configuration-as-code-jcasc","sec":"jenkins-s071","q":"How do you export the existing configuration to <code>jenkins.yaml</code>?","a":"<p>Go to Manage Jenkins, Configuration as Code, View/Download Configuration to export the live config as YAML. Trim it, move secrets to environment variables or <code>${VAR}</code> references, commit it, and point <code>CASC_JENKINS_CONFIG</code> at it so later startups load it.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0086","card":"q-jenkins-071-monitoring-and-troubleshooting","sec":"jenkins-s072","q":"How do you monitor controller health, CPU, memory and thread dumps?","a":"<p>Use the Monitoring (JavaMelody) plugin for CPU, heap and threads, the built-in System Information and Load Statistics, and take thread dumps at <code>/threadDump</code> (or via the CLI) to diagnose hangs. Watch JVM heap, GC, executor queue length and disk.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0087","card":"q-jenkins-071-monitoring-and-troubleshooting","sec":"jenkins-s072","q":"How do you integrate Jenkins metrics with Prometheus and Grafana?","a":"<p>Install the Prometheus metrics plugin, which exposes <code>/prometheus</code> for scraping. Grafana dashboards then show queue length, build durations, executor utilization, node health and JVM stats. Add alerts for queue backlog or controller memory.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0088","card":"q-jenkins-071-monitoring-and-troubleshooting","sec":"jenkins-s072","q":"How do you troubleshoot a hung or deadlocked Jenkins, or heavy GC pauses?","a":"<p>Capture thread dumps (<code>/threadDump</code>) to find blocked or deadlocked threads and heavy operations; capture a heap dump and enable GC logging to spot memory pressure. Common fixes: raise heap (<code>-Xmx</code>), tune GC (for example G1), reduce plugins and executors, move builds off the controller, add build discarders and split load across agents.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0089","card":"q-jenkins-072-disk-hygiene-and-workspace-cleanup","sec":"jenkins-s073","q":"How do you manage disk space on the controller?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Build discarder</strong>: cap builds and artifacts kept per job (<code>buildDiscarder</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Artifact retention</strong>: keep artifacts for fewer builds than logs.</li>\n  <li>Clean workspaces (<code>cleanWs</code>), offload artifacts to external storage (Artifactory, Nexus, S3) and monitor <code>JENKINS_HOME</code> disk.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>options { buildDiscarder(logRotator(numToKeepStr: &#x27;30&#x27;, artifactNumToKeepStr: &#x27;5&#x27;)) }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0090","card":"q-jenkins-072-disk-hygiene-and-workspace-cleanup","sec":"jenkins-s073","q":"How do you clean workspaces after a build?","a":"<p>Use the Workspace Cleanup plugin's <code>cleanWs</code> step, typically in <code>post { always { } }</code>, which frees disk and avoids stale files contaminating later builds.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>post { always { cleanWs() } }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0091","card":"q-jenkins-073-quality-gates-and-vulnerability-scanning","sec":"jenkins-s074","q":"How do you integrate SonarQube and enforce quality gates?","a":"<p>Run the SonarScanner inside <code>withSonarQubeEnv</code>, then wait on the asynchronous quality gate with <code>waitForQualityGate</code> and fail the build if it is not passed.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;SonarQube&#x27;) {\n  steps { withSonarQubeEnv(&#x27;sonar&#x27;) { sh &#x27;mvn sonar:sonar&#x27; } }\n}\nstage(&#x27;Quality Gate&#x27;) {\n  steps { timeout(time: 10, unit: &#x27;MINUTES&#x27;) {\n    waitForQualityGate abortPipeline: true    // fail if gate not met\n  } }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0092","card":"q-jenkins-073-quality-gates-and-vulnerability-scanning","sec":"jenkins-s074","q":"How do you integrate vulnerability scanners (Trivy, Snyk, Dependency-Check)?","a":"<p>Add a security stage that runs the scanner and fails on threshold breaches (for example high or critical CVEs). Publish reports for visibility.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;Scan&#x27;) {\n  steps {\n    sh &#x27;trivy image --exit-code 1 --severity HIGH,CRITICAL registry/app:$BUILD_NUMBER&#x27;\n    // sh &#x27;snyk test --severity-threshold=high&#x27;\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0093","card":"q-jenkins-074-notifications","sec":"jenkins-s075","q":"How do you send Slack, Teams or email notifications by outcome?","a":"<p>Put them in the <code>post</code> block keyed to result conditions, using the Slack, Teams or Email Extension plugins.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>post {\n  success { slackSend channel: &#x27;#ci&#x27;, color: &#x27;good&#x27;,   message: &quot;✅ ${env.JOB_NAME} #${env.BUILD_NUMBER}&quot; }\n  failure { slackSend channel: &#x27;#ci&#x27;, color: &#x27;danger&#x27;, message: &quot;❌ ${env.JOB_NAME} #${env.BUILD_NUMBER} ${env.BUILD_URL}&quot; }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0094","card":"q-jenkins-075-scenario-controller-out-of-memory-from-p","sec":"jenkins-s076","q":"A commit triggers 10 parallel jobs and the controller crashes with OOM. How do you prevent and recover?","a":"<p>Recover by restarting the controller (temporarily with more <code>-Xmx</code>) and aborting the runaway builds. Prevent it by moving builds to agents (controller executors 0), capping concurrency with throttling, <code>disableConcurrentBuilds</code> and per-job or per-label limits, using a Quiet Period to coalesce rapid pushes, right-sizing executors, adding build discarders and alerting on heap and queue. Ephemeral Kubernetes agents keep load off the controller.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0095","card":"q-jenkins-076-scenario-a-pipeline-hangs-on-one-step","sec":"jenkins-s077","q":"A pipeline is stuck on a step indefinitely. How do you debug and safeguard?","a":"<p>Open the build's Console or Pipeline Steps to find the stuck step, take a controller thread dump (<code>/threadDump</code>) and check the agent (process, disk, network). Common culprits: a waiting <code>input</code>, a hung <code>sh</code> subprocess, network calls without timeouts, or an offline agent. Safeguard with <code>timeout</code> on risky steps, <code>retry</code> on flaky ones, an overall pipeline <code>timeout</code> option, and a timeout on <code>input</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>options { timeout(time: 60, unit: &#x27;MINUTES&#x27;) }         // whole-pipeline guard\nsteps { timeout(time: 5, unit: &#x27;MINUTES&#x27;) { sh &#x27;./maybe-hangs.sh&#x27; } }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0096","card":"q-jenkins-077-scenario-helm-deploy-without-embedding-a","sec":"jenkins-s078","q":"How do you deploy to Kubernetes with Helm without cluster-admin credentials in the codebase?","a":"<p>Store the kubeconfig or token as a Jenkins credential (better, use a cloud IAM/OIDC identity or a Vault-issued short-lived token), bind it only for the deploy block with <code>withCredentials</code>, and use a least-privilege service account scoped to the target namespace rather than cluster-admin. Run the deploy on an ephemeral agent and never commit secrets.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>withCredentials([file(credentialsId: &#x27;kubeconfig-prod&#x27;, variable: &#x27;KUBECONFIG&#x27;)]) {\n  sh &#x27;helm upgrade --install app ./chart -n app-prod --atomic --wait&#x27;\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0097","card":"q-jenkins-078-scenario-migrating-200-freestyle-jobs-to","sec":"jenkins-s079","q":"What is the step-by-step strategy?","a":"<ol>\n  <li>Inventory and categorize jobs by pattern (build, deploy, cron).</li>\n  <li>Build templates and Shared Library steps for each common pattern.</li>\n  <li>Pilot-migrate a few representative jobs to Jenkinsfiles in their repos and run them in parallel with the old jobs to compare.</li>\n  <li>Roll out by category, converting configs to declarative plus Multibranch.</li>\n  <li>Standardize credentials through the credentials store and parameterize jobs.</li>\n  <li>Decommission Freestyle jobs once the pipelines are verified.</li>\n  <li>Add JCasC so the setup is reproducible.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Migrate incrementally, never big-bang.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0098","card":"q-jenkins-079-scenario-blue-green-or-canary-deployment","sec":"jenkins-s080","q":"How do you design a zero-downtime blue/green or canary pipeline?","a":"<p><strong>Blue/green</strong>: deploy the new version to an idle green environment, run smoke tests, then switch the load balancer or service from blue to green (rollback is switching back). <strong>Canary</strong>: route a small percentage of traffic to the new version, watch metrics and error rates, then ramp up or roll back. In Jenkins use stages for deploy-green, test, <code>input</code> approval (or an automated metric gate), traffic shift and verify, with automatic rollback on failure.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;Switch traffic&#x27;) {\n  steps {\n    sh &#x27;kubectl apply -f green-deploy.yaml &amp;&amp; ./smoke-test.sh green&#x27;\n    input message: &#x27;Cut over to green?&#x27;, ok: &#x27;Switch&#x27;\n    sh &#x27;kubectl patch service app -p \\&#x27;{&quot;spec&quot;:{&quot;selector&quot;:{&quot;slot&quot;:&quot;green&quot;}}}\\&#x27;&#x27;\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0099","card":"q-jenkins-080-scenario-flaky-scm-checkout","sec":"jenkins-s081","q":"How do you make checkout robust against intermittent network failures?","a":"<p>Wrap <code>checkout</code> in <code>retry</code> and <code>timeout</code>, log each attempt, and use <code>catchError</code> or <code>try</code> to surface diagnostics. Shallow clones and reasonable Git timeouts also reduce flakiness.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;Checkout&#x27;) {\n  steps {\n    retry(3) {\n      timeout(time: 5, unit: &#x27;MINUTES&#x27;) {\n        echo &quot;Checkout attempt at ${new Date()}&quot;\n        checkout scm\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false}];
